 3. The Output: California, China and the U.K. 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Here's our guide to catch you up on the AI policy news you may have missed this week:

 🐻 California bill watch: Gov. Gavin Newsom (D) signed multiple pieces of AI legislation into law this week, including measures on kids' safety.

 A new chatbot law imposes time limits for minors and requires safety disclosures and safeguards around self-harm. OpenAI endorsed the bill at the end of August. Another measure places a four-year moratorium on the sale of AI companion toys to children under 16. On Wednesday, Newsom also signed two bills establishing a framework for independent organizations to verify AI systems and creating a state registry for AI auditors. OpenAI endorsed both measures the same day Newsom signed them into law. 🇬🇧 Superintelligence ban pitched: British lawmaker Alex Sobel introduced a bill drafted by the advocacy group ControlAI that would "prohibit the development, deployment and operation of artificial superintelligence systems."

 🇨🇳 China rejects claims: Beijing pushed back after U.S. intelligence and cybersecurity agencies said DeepSeek, Moonshot AI, Alibaba and other China-based AI companies "are engaging in aggressive, malicious, and targeted distillation activities at an industrial scale."

 Distillation — the process of training a smaller AI model using the outputs of a larger model — has become a growing flashpoint in the U.S.-China AI race. 🤖 Rogue agents targeted: Reps. Josh Gottheimer (D-N.J.) and Mike Lawler (R-N.Y.) officially introduced bipartisan legislation this week that would set federal standards for detecting and stopping unauthorized AI agents. 

 Our Axios colleague Sam Sabin first reported on the bill last week. 👀 AI surveillance spreads: State-run surveillance operations around the world are using Claude to streamline their operations, Anthropic said in a threat report released yesterday.

 
 
